    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Mathematics is one of my favorite subjects. I find it both fascinating and satisfying to solve problems. I enjoy the logical flow and the way every solution fits together. Even though I don’t always understand every step, I keep coming back because there’s always a way to break it down. I often use math in everyday life, from budgeting to cooking.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, sometimes. Especially when a problem is complicated or if I haven’t practiced it recently. It’s like my brain gets stuck in a loop. I tend to panic and then it gets worse. But I’ve learned to breathe and take it one step at a time. Yoga helps a lot, especially during exam weeks.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used an AI tutor app to help with algebra. It explained problems clearly and gave me instant feedback. I appreciated how it broke down each step, even if I was still confused at first. It wasn’t perfect, but it helped me understand the concepts better. I think AI can be really useful, especially for visual learners.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a). Plug in the values of a, b, and c. Calculate the discriminant under the square root. If it’s positive, you get two real solutions. If zero, one solution. If negative, complex solutions.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "First, take the derivative of the function f(x), which is f'(x). Then, set f'(x) equal to zero and solve for x. These x-values are the potential stationary points. Plug them back into the original function to find the corresponding y-values. Also, check if the derivative changes sign around those points to classify them as maxima, minima, or points of inflection.",
